toms worsened' (score=0.74):]\n  Source: Symptomatic pneumatosis intestinalis (including portal venous gas) after laparoscopic total colectomy. > Differential Diagnosis\n  Other pathologies that are part of the differential diagnosis must be ruled out. One of the more serious conditions is bowel ischemia. Infarction must be considered as a differential diagnosis, especi\n[RAG context for 'He feels that his symptoms are exacerbated with large fatty meals and when he drinks alcohol' (score=0.77):]\n  Source: Infection increases mortality in necrotizing pancreatitis: A systematic review and meta-analysis. > Evaluation\n  An alcoholic cause should be considered in individuals with a history of chronic heavy alcohol use, typically defined as 4 to 5 drinks daily for at least 5 years. These patients often lack biliary pat\n[RAG context for 'He feels that his symptoms are exacerbated with large fatty meals and when he drinks alcohol' (score=0.79):]\n  Source: Oktoberfest binge drinking and acute pancreatitis: is there really no relationship? > Differential Diagnosis\n  There is a broad differential diagnosis for this condition, as conditions affecting the gallbladder, stomach, or upper GI tract may present with a similar pain pattern. A history of frequent alcohol u\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  He feels that his symptoms are exacerbated with large fatty meals and when he drinks alcohol → mesenteric ischemia exacerbation by dietary/alcohol triggers → Chronic Mesenteric Ischemia with Superimposed Acute Episode\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for chronic mesenteric ischemia with acute exacerbation, particularly given the patient's diabetes and vascular risk factors\n  The patient has a medical history of poorly managed diabetes → atherosclerosis as a risk factor for chronic mesenteric ischemia → Chronic Mesenteric Ischemia with Superimposed Acute Episode\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Assess for chronic mesenteric ischemia secondary to atherosclerosis, given diabetes as a major risk factor\n  constipation → impacted stool-induced bowel obstruction → Impacted Stool-Induced Bowel Obstruction\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Consider impacted stool as the mechanism of obstruction, especially with chronic constipation history\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
